A built XPCWhatsMeowService.xpc is GPL v3
-----------------------------------------

The service statically links whatsmeow and its dependencies, fetched by Go at build time
(see Go/wabridge/go.mod and go.sum — none of them is redistributed in this repository).
Two of them are copyleft:

| Module                | Licence                           |
|-----------------------|-----------------------------------|
| go.mau.fi/libsignal   | GPL v3                            |
| go.mau.fi/whatsmeow   | MPL 2.0                           |
| go.mau.fi/util        | MPL 2.0                           |
| everything else       | MIT, BSD, ISC or Apache 2.0       |

libsignal decides the outcome: the service binary is a combined work that includes it, so
the **binary** must be distributed under the GPL v3, whatever the licence of the sources
that went into it. MIT and MPL 2.0 are both compatible with that. Shipping the .xpc inside
an application means distributing that binary, with the obligations that carries: keep its
notices, ship the licence texts, and make the corresponding source available. The service
bundle carries the texts itself (Contents/Resources: GPL-3.0.txt, MPL-2.0.txt,
ThirdPartyLicenses.txt, NOTICE), and this repository, being public, is the source.

ThirdPartyLicenses.txt is generated from go.sum by scripts/build-xcframework.sh and lists
every module linked into the service, with its version and licence text, plus the Go
runtime's.


Nothing else contains whatsmeow
-------------------------------

The XPCWhatsMeow and XPCWhatsMeowProtocol modules — the parts an application links — contain
no whatsmeow, libsignal or Go code at all. An application talks to the service over XPC:
messages between two processes, carrying JSON. No shared address space, no shared data
structures, no whatsmeow headers or types anywhere in the client.

That is the line the GPL itself draws. The FSF puts the boundary of a program at the address
space: modules linked into one executable are one program, while pipes, sockets and messages
between processes are "communication mechanisms normally used between two separate
programs". This project is built to sit on the far side of that line, and you can check it on
the binaries:

    nm YourApp.app/Contents/MacOS/YourApp | grep -c 'go.mau.fi'                                  # 0
    nm YourApp.app/Contents/XPCServices/XPCWhatsMeowService.xpc/Contents/MacOS/XPCWhatsMeowService \
        | grep -c 'go.mau.fi/libsignal'                                                         # hundreds

So an application that uses this service does not have to publish its own source. It ships a
GPL program beside a proprietary one, which is the ordinary case of shipping any GPL tool with
your software.

Do not link XPCWhatsMeowServiceKit into an application. It exists for the service (and this
package's tests); linking it anywhere else puts libsignal in that binary.


WhatsApp
--------

WhatsApp offers no official API for personal accounts. whatsmeow implements the protocol
WhatsApp's own web and desktop clients use, as a linked companion device. WhatsApp's terms
do not permit unofficial clients, and WhatsApp may restrict or ban accounts that use them —
particularly accounts that send in bulk or to people who have not messaged them. That is a
risk for you and your users to weigh; no licence covers it.

